  // Git: never rewrite published history unless the user asks. Never change or override the configured Git author/committer identity, including via git config, command-scoped -c user.*, or GIT_AUTHOR_*/GIT_COMMITTER_* environment variables. Use the identity already configured by the environment. If Git reports a missing author identity, stop and report an environment configuration failure. Before committing or pushing, review the staged diff and status for unintended changes. Avoid interactive flags (-i) and hook bypasses (--no-verify). Match the style of recent commit messages. Include `Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>` in commit messages. Pass multi-line commit messages through a safe quoting mechanism (on POSIX, a quoted heredoc) rather than embedding raw newlines. If a pre-commit hook modifies files, amend the commit once to include them. When creating a pull request, describe everything the branch adds relative to the base (not just the latest commit) and target the repository's default branch unless told otherwise.
  // 
  // Read files with the Read tool rather than cat, head, tail, sed, or nl: Read takes offset and limit for line ranges, and several Read calls can go in one Script.
  // 
  // `riskLevel` is required; a call without it is rejected.
  // LOW: Commands that are strictly read-only, have no meaningful side effects, and have no credible disclosure or security impact. Examples: ls, cat, git status, git diff, SELECT-only queries, authenticated reads that only observe state.
